<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Some review</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find antiderivatives for the following functions.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.15in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds \int\frac{x^2}{(x^3+8)^3}~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Let's try a substitution:  <m>u=(x^3+8)</m> so that
          <m>du=3x^2~dx</m>.  Then
          <md>
            \int\frac{x^2}{(x^3+8)^3}~dx = \frac13\int u^{-3}~du =
            -\frac16u^{-2}=-\frac16\frac1{(x^3+8)^2} + C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.15in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds \int xe^{-x^2}~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Let's try a substitution:  <m>u=-x^2</m> so that
          <m>du=-2x~dx</m>.  Then
          <md>
            \int xe^{-x^2}~dx = -\frac12\int e^u~du =
            -\frac12 e^u=-\frac12 e^{-x^2} + C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.15in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds \int x^2\cos(x)~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Let's try integration by parts:
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u=x^2 \amp du=2x~dx \\
            dv = \cos(x)~dx \amp v=\sin(x) \\
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            \int x^2\cos(x)~dx = x^2\sin(x)-2\int x\sin(x)~dx
          </md>
          so we need to integrate by parts again:
          <md>
            \int x^2\cos(x)~dx = x^2\sin(x)+2x\cos(x)-2\sin(x)+C
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds \int \frac1{1+x^2}~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Yay! This is on the table of antiderivatives:
          <md>
            \int \frac1{1+x^2}~dx = \arctan(x)+C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Remember that we find the area of a region by slicing in some
        convenient way.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the area of the region between the graph <m>y=e^x</m>,
          the line <m>y=1</m>, and the line <m>x=3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
            A = \int_0^3 (e^x-1)~dx = (e^x-x)|_0^3 = e^3-4
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the area of the region between the curve <m>x=y^3-3y</m>
          and the lines <m>x=4</m>, <m>y=-2</m> and <m>y=2</m>.
        </p>
        <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
          <shortdescription>The region between a cubic curve and three
          lines</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="pf-11-volume-1">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/11-volume-1.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Slicing the region horizontally, we have
          <md>
            A = \int_{-2}^2 \left(4-(y^3-3y)\right)~dy = 8
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that the region in the previous question is spun
          about the line <m>y=4</m>.  Set an integral that finds the
          resulting volume.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          The radius of a cross section is <m>4-(y^3-3y)</m> so
          <md>
            dV = \pi(4-(y^3-3y))^2~dy
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            V = \pi\int_{-2}^2 \left(4-(y^3-3y)\right)^2~dy = 326.0
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's consider the function
        <md>F(x)=\int_0^x \cos(t^2)~dt</md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>F(0)</m>, <m>F(1)</m>, and <m>F(2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          The first is straightforward: <m>F(0)=\int_0^0\cos(t^2)~dt =
          0</m>. For the others, we can use Desmos:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              F(1)\amp = \int_0^1\cos(t^2)~dt = 0.905
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              F(2)\amp = \int_0^2\cos(t^2)~dt = 0.461
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without drawing a graph of <m>F(x)</m>, determine whether
          <m>F</m> is increasing or decreasing at <m>x=1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          Since <m>F'(x)=\cos(x^2)</m>, we see that
          <m>F'(1)=\cos(1^2) = 0.54 \gt 0</m> so <m>F</m> is
          increasing there.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the first critical point of <m>F</m> to the right of
          <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="11">
        <p>
          <m>F'(x)=\cos(x^2) = 0</m> when <m>x^2=\frac \pi2</m> so
          <m>x=\sqrt{\pi/2} = 1.25</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Here's an antiderivative we'll need later in the course:
        <md>
          \int \frac1{x^2-x}~dx = \int\frac{1}{x(x-1)}~dx
        </md>.
        Neither substitution or integration by parts will work, but we
        can simplify the function we're antidifferentiating by finding
        constants <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> such that
        <md>
          \frac{1}{x(x-1)} = \frac Ax + \frac B{x-1}
        </md>.
        To do this, start with the right side and find a common
        denominator and then set the numerators equal.  Then find
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> and the resulting antiderivative.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution componet="11">
      <p>
        <md>
          \frac Ax + \frac B{x-1} = \frac{A(x-1) + Bx}{x(x-1)} =
          \frac1{x(x-1)}
        </md>,
        which means that
        <md>
          A(x-1) + Bx = 1
        </md>.
        This has to be true for all <m>x</m> so when <m>x=1</m>, we
        have <m>B=1</m> and when <m>x=0</m>, we have <m>A=-1</m>.
        Therefore,
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int \frac1{x^2-x}~dx \amp = \int\frac{1}{x(x-1)}~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \int \left(-\frac1x + \frac1{x-1}\right)~dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = -\ln|x| + \ln|x+1|
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \ln\left|\frac{x+1}{x}\right|
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
